\title{AT-FD-7 · Actual augmented shapes and their diagram model}
\taxon{Acceptance test}
\meta{id}{AT-FD-7}
\meta{area}{foundation feasibility}
\meta{origin}{design/11-foundation-feasibility.md}
\meta{status}{proposed}
\meta{statement-version}{1}
\tag{acceptance-test}
\tag{area:fd}
\tag{status:proposed}

\p{Define the exact generating incidence data, operations, and equations of the set-level augmented VDC. Build the free/arity presentation with the hypotheses actually used by its nerve theorem. Validate \code{c₁^∅}, \code{(0,0)} cells and both their compositions, nullary inputs, mixed rows, and shared sides. Prove the no-horizontal fragment recovers 2-categories, including composition laws. Reject the attempted ordinary active \code{[0] → [1]} encoding of a one-input/empty-output transition. Supply the diagram model required by \code{MapRel} and \code{Mod}: Reedy/elegance if that route works, or a concrete alternative with the necessary cofibrant-source, relative restriction, and replacement results. A fallback is accepted only with those results. Prove any canonical encoding used by the prototype; flags or drawings alone are not an arity or normalization theorem.}
\p{\strong{Depends on:} minimal category/arity theory from the inventory. This gate supplies the shape prerequisites for \ref{at-fd-0003} and \ref{at-fd-0008}; it is not allowed to rely on them for the definition of its own category. \strong{Evidence:} algebra, shape presentation, nerve hypotheses, fragment comparison, and actual model lemmas. \strong{Failure response:} revise the presentation; if no workable augmented shape/model route exists, stop the current root track and produce a model-change decision.}
\p{Named by: \ref{dec-ch-0018}, \ref{dec-kr-0015}, \ref{dec-kr-0016}, \ref{dec-kr-0017}, \ref{dec-kr-0018}, \ref{dec-rt-0016}, \ref{dec-rt-0030}, \ref{dec-tl-0021}.}
\p{\strong{Partial implementation evidence:} \code{Kernel/Augmented/} implements incident generating cells, shared-side rows, concatenation laws, primitive operation types and both derived vertical-cell compositions. \code{Simplex.no_active_zero_to_one} and \code{Simplex.no_active_encoding} prove the ordinary-active obstruction. \code{FromTwoCategory.cellEquiv} and its identity, associativity and interchange laws verify the binary cell model of a supplied strict 2-category; \code{FromCat.cellNatTransEquiv} identifies the supplied Cat model with natural transformations and preserves both binary compositions. Concrete examples cover mixed rows, nullary input, nonidentity outside sides and the rejected intermediate-arrow mismatch. Names here are under \code{Kernel.Augmented}.}
\p{\strong{Algebra evidence:} \ref{augmented-algebra} records all five equation families in \code{Kernel.Augmented.Laws}, their explicit incident domains and the complete-boundary equality proofs. \code{Kernel.Augmented.AdditiveModel.algebra} proves them for arbitrary additive commutative cell labels over any supplied vertical category and horizontal graph. Mixed nested examples retain distinct cells; invalid intermediate factorizations and empty inner rows are rejected. The equation fields are obligations on arbitrary operation data, with a checked nontrivial model, rather than unconditional claims about all operations.}
\p{\strong{Still open:} extending the supplied binary 2-category model to the full augmented algebra, the general no-horizontal algebra/discrete-nerve comparison, free/arity and nerve presentation, any canonical encoding theorem, and all required labelled diagram-model results. This partial evidence does not pass or weaken the current gate; status remains proposed and statement-version remains 1.}
\p{\em{Reproduction:} \code{docs/augmented/README.md} pins Definition 1.2 and Example 1.5 of Koudenburg arXiv:1910.11189v4 and lists checked declarations and remaining obligations. \code{lake build Augmented} and \code{python3 scripts/check_foundations.py} compile and transitively audit the entire new namespace against the approved axioms; \code{docs/foundations/inventory.json} records exact types, source hashes, dependencies and axiom closures.}
